<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TestPlatformRoot Condition="$(TestPlatformRoot) == ''">..\..\..\</TestPlatformRoot>
    <TestProject>true</TestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(TestPlatformRoot)scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets" />
  <PropertyGroup Label="Configuration">
    <RootNamespace>Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector.UnitTests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    DO NOT ADD <TargetFramework> (singular) BESIDE <TargetFrameworks> (plural) BELOW.

    This project set BOTH from the commit that created it (468d4b232, 2017-09-07) until c38eef703.
    For that whole time its 35 tests NEVER RAN - not "stopped running", never ran at all. The build
    was green, the test step was green, and a .trx was produced and published on every single run.

    MECHANISM. With both properties present MSBuild cannot tell an outer build from an inner build,
    so the project builds as if it were single-targeted but does not import the per-inner-build
    assets from NuGet's build/ folder. MSTest.TestAdapter ships the adapter that way. The assembly
    compiled and ran, and vstest found no adapter in it, so it discovered zero tests.

    HOW IT HID FOR NINE YEARS - this is the part worth keeping:

      the .trx existed, was well-formed, and was published    so "no results" never looked like a gap
      Counters/@total = 0 with 0 result nodes                 an empty run is not a failed run
      the only signal was a <RunInfo> warning inside the file  which nothing read
      of 54 test projects this was the ONLY one with both     16 of 17 in the same folder were fine

    A green test step means the tests that ran passed. It does not mean any tests ran.

    VERIFIED, both directions and in CI on a clean restore (build 3064664):

      property present  ->  adapter absent   ->  total=0,  RunInfos: "No test is available"
      property removed  ->  adapter present  ->  total=35, 33 passed, 1 failed, 1 NotExecuted
      declared [TestMethod] 35  ==  discovered 35            nothing is left behind the adapter

    The 1 failure is real and older than this fix - see the comment in EventLogDataCollectorTests.cs.
    It is left failing on purpose. Making these tests run was the change; deciding a 2017 product
    question was not.

    16.11 carried the identical defect in the same file and fixed it the same way (36 declared,
    36 discovered). If you are looking at a third branch, check this file there before assuming
    its EventLogCollector tests have ever executed.

    HOW TO CHECK THAT THE SINGULAR PROPERTY IS REALLY GONE - because a text search will lie to you.
    This comment necessarily writes the token it warns about, so grepping the file for it MATCHES
    THIS PARAGRAPH. A reader asking "is the duplicate still here" gets a hit, and the hit is the
    sentence saying it was removed.

    Ask the XML instead. Element counts are properties of the document and no amount of prose can
    move them:

      [xml](Get-Content <this file> -Raw).SelectNodes('//TargetFramework')    -> 0 elements
      [xml](Get-Content <this file> -Raw).SelectNodes('//TargetFrameworks')   -> 1 element

    Deliberately NO count of text matches is stated here. 16.11 tried that on their copy and the
    number changed three times in three drafts - writing the explanation added mentions, and
    rewriting it to remove the number changed it again. A count of occurrences that lives inside the
    text being counted cannot stay true, because the act of explaining it alters what it counts.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net451</TargetFrameworks>
    <AssemblyName>Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector.UnitTests</AssemblyName>    
    <WarningsAsErrors>true</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\DataCollectors\Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector\Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="System.Runtime" />
    <Reference Include="System" />
    <Reference Include="Microsoft.CSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <Import Project="$(TestPlatformRoot)scripts\build\TestPlatform.targets" />
</Project>
